{"name":"Penny","hex":"#a97f5a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Penny","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A diminutive of the female given name Penelope."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname.","A place name, presumably all taken from the surname:\nAn unincorporated community in Calloway County, Kentucky, United States.\nAn unincorporated community in Pike County, Kentucky.\nA small community on the Fraser River in central British Columbia, Canada."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Penny"}],"entries":[{"title":"Penny","blocks":[{"text":"A penny is a coin or a unit of currency in various countries. Borrowed from the Carolingian denarius, it is usually the smallest denomination within a currency system. At present, it is the formal name of the British penny unit and the de facto name of the American one-cent coin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/1911-Australian-Penny-Reverse.jpg/330px-1911-Australian-Penny-Reverse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penny","wikidata":"Q234129"},{"title":"Penny (United States coin)","blocks":[{"text":"The penny, officially \"one cent\", is a United States coin worth one cent, equal to 1⁄100 of a dollar ($0.01). It has been the lowest face-value coin or banknote of U.S. currency since the abolition of the half-cent coin in 1857. Introduced in 1793, it has only been produced for collectors since 2026."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/US_One_Cent_Obv.png/330px-US_One_Cent_Obv.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penny_(United_States_coin)","wikidata":"Q161604"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Coin_of_Eric_Bloodaxe_Norse_king_of_York_952_954.jpg/330px-Coin_of_Eric_Bloodaxe_Norse_king_of_York_952_954.jpg?utm